1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,460
In 1898, the leaders of the separate communities of New York signed documents to consolidate into greater New York City.

2
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,540
But a piece of paper can only do so much to unite the people of a city.

3
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:18,120
To do this, it would take a massive underground system that connects the sprawling metropolis above.

4
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:23,220
I'm Dave Dinkins, and welcome back to Modern Marvel's New York City Week.

5
00:00:23,221 --> 00:00:30,220
On any workday, more than 30% of all those using mass transit in the entire country are

6
00:00:30,221 --> 00:00:33,340
riding New York City subways and buses.

7
00:00:33,341 --> 00:00:35,280
Stand clear at the closing doors.

8
00:00:35,281 --> 00:00:39,140
The New York City subway leaves right now, here on the History Channel.

9
00:00:53,220 --> 00:01:07,220
work on the new york subway system began at the turn of the century in the time of tremendous

10
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:17,560
change in america in the four years it took to build the original nine mile line immigration

11
00:01:17,561 --> 00:01:22,760
would more than double reaching nearly a million people a year the greatest wave of immigrants

12
00:01:22,761 --> 00:01:24,500
ever to come to U.S. shores.

13
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:28,400
For most, the first stop was New York City.

14
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,320
By 1904, the city's population had swelled to a size second only to London.

15
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:43,300
While other cities built outward, New York built upward as well,

16
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:45,980
becoming the first city of skyscrapers.

17
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,180
Down under the streets, subway construction continued,

18
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,260
and two more bridges were opened across the East River,

19
00:01:52,261 --> 00:01:53,680
linking New York to Brooklyn.

20
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:58,700
Central Park, only recently a wilderness far to the north of the city,

21
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,760
soon became a popular recreational area.

22
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:07,940
In those days, motor cars were just a curiosity, not real transportation.

23
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,480
Henry Ford's Model T was still half a decade in the future.

24
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:18,400
In 1903, the Wright brothers made their first successful powered flight

25
00:02:18,401 --> 00:02:19,820
at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.

26
00:02:19,821 --> 00:02:25,880
though two years later a local aviator at Coney Island still hadn't got it right.

27
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:30,220
The dynamic Teddy Roosevelt was president.

28
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:34,780
Perhaps more than any individual, he symbolized the spirit of the age.

29
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:38,460
Charismatic, he was followed everywhere by photographers,

30
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:40,680
making him the first media president.

31
00:02:40,681 --> 00:02:47,560
on October 27th

32
00:02:47,561 --> 00:02:49,660
1904 the press was on hand

33
00:02:49,661 --> 00:02:51,860
for the grand opening of the New York subway

34
00:02:51,861 --> 00:02:53,660
these scenes were captured

35
00:02:53,661 --> 00:02:55,920
by Edison's famous cameraman and director

36
00:02:55,921 --> 00:02:57,020
Edwin S. Porter

37
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:21,540
Many thought this new nine-mile line would solve New York's congestion problems once and for all.

38
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:28,520
But William Barclay Parsons, the engineer who designed and supervised the subway, said something quite different.

39
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:33,600
The instant that this line is finished, there will arise a demand for other lines.

40
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:35,320
Parsons was right.

41
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:41,400
Over the years, his original nine-mile line has grown into a 238-mile system.

42
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:49,520
By some measures, it is not the world's biggest subway.

43
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:51,560
Tokyo's carries more passengers.

44
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:54,180
Try telling that to these people.

45
00:03:54,181 --> 00:04:06,840
but as many transit professionals will attest new york system is the world's greatest it has

46
00:04:06,841 --> 00:04:13,320
more cars stations and miles of track it runs more trains and employs more people

47
00:04:13,321 --> 00:04:25,820
its lost property office deep beneath the 8th Avenue subway could stock a small town department store

48
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:51,480
The Transit Police Force is the second largest police force in New York State, seventh largest

49
00:04:51,481 --> 00:05:04,480
in the nation.

50
00:05:04,481 --> 00:05:10,160
Today's New York subway trains could hold the entire population of Boston at one time.

51
00:05:10,161 --> 00:05:17,560
And if they were kind enough to remain standing, all 300,000 residents of Omaha could have seats.

52
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:49,540
Vast underground track junctions dwarf the largest freeway interchanges in both size and complexity.

53
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:21,280
so

54
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:54,160
New York City's innovative subway lines offer four tracks, two for local and two for express,

55
00:06:57,140 --> 00:06:59,720
creating less congestion and a faster ride.

56
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:02,180
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

57
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:09,420
Yet in these vintage photographs, Broadway often appears calm, almost idyllic.

58
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:16,160
But then, back in the 1800s, photographic plates required bright light and long exposures.

59
00:07:16,161 --> 00:07:23,160
So photographers often shot at high noon on sunny summer days when traffic was at a minimum.

60
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:37,160
This is what the streets were really like in the late 1800s.

61
00:07:37,161 --> 00:07:43,080
We can travel from New York halfway to Philadelphia in less time than the length of Broadway.

62
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:44,240
New York Tribune.

63
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:54,280
At certain hours of the day, the whole system of passenger transit can be compared to nothing save that of a mob in locomotion.

64
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:55,760
New York State Engineer.

65
00:07:57,060 --> 00:08:02,380
Modern martyrdom may be succinctly defined as riding in a New York City omnibus.

66
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:03,860
The New York Herald.

67
00:08:03,861 --> 00:08:09,800
And a great deal of the congestion was the fault of one man, William Marcy Tweed.

68
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:11,580
Boss Tweed.

69
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:16,640
By skillfully manipulating the Democratic Party's Tammany Hall political machine,

70
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:20,540
Tweed had established an empire of unprecedented power and corruption

71
00:08:20,541 --> 00:08:23,320
and accumulated an immense personal fortune.

72
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:27,740
Among his key supporters were the many Manhattan horse car operators.

73
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:31,520
In return for illegal payments or protection money,

74
00:08:31,521 --> 00:08:38,520
Tweed defended their franchise against competition from any of the newfangled rapid transit schemes.

75
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:54,520
For there was no shortage of rapid transit schemes.

76
00:08:54,521 --> 00:08:57,520
Periodicals of the day carried dozens of proposals,

77
00:08:57,521 --> 00:09:01,520
Some elegant, some complex, some downright bizarre.

78
00:09:01,521 --> 00:09:06,520
The network of overhead cables was to be driven from underground steam engines.

79
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:12,520
This car was slung below the locomotive to avoid the smoke.

80
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:20,520
Most systems were designed to keep passengers well clear of interference from surface traffic.

81
00:09:20,521 --> 00:09:25,520
It is not clear why this proposal placed the ropes overhead, but the cars on the surface.

82
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:35,480
This one featured a moving walkway, equipped with benches for taking longer journeys and cabins in case of bad weather.

83
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:40,540
Proposals called for an arcade spanning Broadway.

84
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:45,860
Ornamentation was thought to answer potential objections from adjacent property owners.

85
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:54,240
Though this proposal may look just as far-fetched as the others, it became the forerunner of Manhattan's vast network of elevated lines.

86
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:58,800
Inventor Charles Harvey may have succeeded in slipping it past Boss Tweed

87
00:09:58,801 --> 00:10:00,760
because he also had friends in high places,

88
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:04,020
or maybe Tweed just thought it was too harebrained to bother with.

89
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:08,460
In any event, Harvey obtained a permit to build his elevated railroad north

90
00:10:08,461 --> 00:10:10,280
along Greenwich Street from the Battery.

91
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:14,240
Harvey personally tested his invention in 1867.

92
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:18,720
It was the first successful cable-powered passenger railway in the world,

93
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:22,320
predating the first San Francisco cable car by five years.

94
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:27,700
Regular passenger service began in the summer of 1868.

95
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:29,860
Operation was unreliable.

96
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:32,460
The cable mechanism reportedly was noisy.

97
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:36,760
And after only one year of operation, the line was temporarily shut down.

98
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:43,520
Meanwhile, on New York's east side, Alfred Beach had an idea of his own.

99
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:48,560
Something of a renaissance man, Beach was publisher of The Sun, New York's most popular Penny Daly,

100
00:10:48,561 --> 00:10:51,260
and editor of The Scientific American, then a weekly.

101
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:54,680
Among other things, he invented the first practical typewriter.

102
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:57,280
He was also a distinguished patent attorney

103
00:10:57,281 --> 00:10:59,600
and counted among his friends Samuel Morse,

104
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:02,380
Alexander Graham Bell, and Thomas Edison.

105
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:05,860
Beach and Scientific American had long been active

106
00:11:05,861 --> 00:11:08,080
in promoting rapid transit for the city.

107
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:13,880
In 1867, Beach demonstrated a simple passenger-carrying pneumatic tube

108
00:11:13,881 --> 00:11:15,840
at the exhibition of the American Institute.

109
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:19,900
Now, he was thinking about how to build a pneumatic subway.

110
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:32,680
Beach knew that he couldn't get a bill permitting him to build a passenger line

111
00:11:32,681 --> 00:11:34,280
past the Tweed-controlled governor,

112
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:39,220
so instead he got a permit to build a small underground pneumatic postal dispatch line.

113
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:44,820
Later, without Tweed noticing, he got permission to enlarge the size of the tube.

114
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:51,000
He began tunneling from the basement of Devlin's Clothing Store at Warren and Broadway.

115
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:53,980
The entire operation was carried on secretly.

116
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:02,680
The hydraulic shield, beached designed to dig the tunnel, is still an industry standard.

117
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:11,400
It forces its way through the soft ground by pushing against the brick walls of the tunnel already in place.

118
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:15,860
At night, dirt was hauled away in wagons rolling on muffled wheels.

119
00:12:15,861 --> 00:12:27,260
It took a team of six tunnelers just 58 nights to dig a tunnel 312 foot long running under Broadway from Murray to Warren streets directly in front of City Hall.

120
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:34,180
From time to time they would check their progress by pushing jointed iron rods past all the underground utilities to the pavement.

121
00:12:38,460 --> 00:12:43,620
A single tubular car seating 22 ran on rails laid inside the tunnel.

122
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:49,340
Its close fit with the tunnel walls allowed it to be blown down the tunnel like a sail before the wind.

123
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:54,600
A 100-horsepower steam engine connected to a large blower provided the push.

124
00:12:55,340 --> 00:12:59,760
When the car reached Murray Street, a telegraph wire rang a bell in the machine room,

125
00:12:59,761 --> 00:13:05,060
and flap valves reversed the direction of the airflow so it was sucked gently back to the station.

126
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:08,980
The air was exhausted to the surface through a grate.

127
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:14,480
Reports at the time indicated that scenes similar to this one were not uncommon when the car was running.

128
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:20,200
Beach unveiled his secret subway to the public February 28, 1870.

129
00:13:20,201 --> 00:13:24,700
He had taken great pains to ensure his station and car were bright and attractive.

130
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:31,080
By year end, more than 400,000 people had paid 25 cents to see or ride it.

131
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:37,160
Tweed immediately tried to close the line, but his power in New York was waning.

132
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:40,400
Over the next several years, the most he and his cronies could do

133
00:13:40,401 --> 00:13:43,840
was to keep Beech from gaining authorization to extend the line.

134
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:50,400
During this time, Tweed also failed in an attempt to tear down Harvey's Elevated.

135
00:13:50,580 --> 00:13:52,840
The new backers scrapped the cable propulsion system

136
00:13:52,841 --> 00:13:55,360
in favor of small enclosed steam locomotives,

137
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:58,720
and the New York Elevated Railway Company began to prosper.

138
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:03,500
It was at this opportune time that another major player stepped forward

139
00:14:03,501 --> 00:14:05,640
on the rapid transit stage, Rufus Gilbert.

140
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:10,100
In 1872, Gilbert had proposed a pneumatic elevated,

141
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:12,640
similar in operating principles to Beech's subway.

142
00:14:13,860 --> 00:14:18,740
With Tweed's power now all but gone, Gilbert got a charter for his pneumatic L,

143
00:14:19,080 --> 00:14:22,340
but allowed himself to be manipulated into giving up control of his company

144
00:14:22,341 --> 00:14:26,220
to men determined to use steam locomotives like Harvey's to pull the trains.

145
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:32,780
In 1877, both Gilbert and Harvey's New York Elevated

146
00:14:32,781 --> 00:14:35,840
began a frenzy of construction northward through Manhattan.

147
00:14:36,580 --> 00:14:39,180
Gilbert built along 2nd and 6th Avenues.

148
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:41,900
New York elevated along 3rd and 9th.

149
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:45,260
In the far northern reaches of Manhattan,

150
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:48,780
the lines were built through land that was almost entirely undeveloped.

151
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:51,160
They were quite literally rural elevators.

152
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:56,040
Downtown, the elevators fulfilled everyone's worst expectations,

153
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:00,560
darkening city skies as they went clattering by at all hours of day and night.

154
00:15:02,780 --> 00:15:16,780
The Elevateds did what they were built to do.

155
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:23,000
They enabled New York to grow from less than a million inhabitants after the Civil War

156
00:15:23,001 --> 00:15:31,440
to three and a half million by the turn of the century.

157
00:15:31,441 --> 00:15:38,440
They developed farmlands into suburbs and suburbs into city neighborhoods.

158
00:15:42,540 --> 00:15:49,540
They were not without their charms.

159
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:15,440
But some might say they did their job too well.

160
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:24,000
By the 1890s, overcrowding on the elevators was described in language

161
00:16:24,001 --> 00:16:28,440
that a generation before had been used to describe the horse cars and omnibuses.

162
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:38,040
A BMT-IND-style train cannot fit into an IRT tunnel

163
00:16:38,041 --> 00:16:40,240
because the trains are longer and wider.

164
00:16:40,860 --> 00:16:45,400
An IRT train can fit into a BMT-IND tunnel, but it's unsafe.

165
00:16:45,820 --> 00:16:50,160
This is because the IRT did not want any competitor's train to run on their tracks.

166
00:16:50,860 --> 00:16:52,540
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

167
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:59,900
It may be taken as a settled fact that the problem of rapid transit for this city

168
00:16:59,901 --> 00:17:02,780
has not been solved by the elevated railroads

169
00:17:02,781 --> 00:17:07,080
and that these structures cannot be permitted to remain permanently in the streets.

170
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:09,240
New York Times, 1886.

171
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:19,680
In 1894, New York's on-again, off-again Board of Rapid Transit Commissioners

172
00:17:19,681 --> 00:17:24,320
met and appointed 35-year-old William Barclay Parsons as its chief engineer.

173
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:29,560
While Boss Tweed had squelched progress in New York,

174
00:17:29,561 --> 00:17:33,560
other cities around the world had built a range of modern rapid transit systems.

175
00:17:33,561 --> 00:17:37,560
Parsons set out to see them for himself.

176
00:17:37,561 --> 00:17:41,560
He studied the most up-to-date systems in Europe,

177
00:17:41,561 --> 00:17:45,560
as well as a new system under construction in Boston.

178
00:17:53,560 --> 00:17:57,560
Parsons submitted his recommendations

179
00:17:57,561 --> 00:17:59,840
to the commission at the end of 1894,

180
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:03,240
and then he went to China.

181
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:07,200
Parsons, a can-do type of guy,

182
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:10,480
was frustrated by all the financial and political maneuvering

183
00:18:10,481 --> 00:18:12,520
that goes into the building of a subway system.

184
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
So while others handled these details in New York,

185
00:18:16,420 --> 00:18:19,220
Parsons spent most of 1898 and 99

186
00:18:19,221 --> 00:18:22,360
surveying the route of a 1,000-mile railroad

187
00:18:22,361 --> 00:18:24,320
from Canton to Hankal.

188
00:18:24,321 --> 00:18:31,460
When he returned to New York in 1899, the commission was ready to begin construction.

189
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:39,400
After the official groundbreaking at City Hall, Parsons lifted a pick to start the actual construction at Bleeker and Green Streets.

190
00:18:40,120 --> 00:18:44,460
Many of the onlookers were not dignitaries, but men hoping for work.

191
00:18:44,461 --> 00:18:49,620
The route the line was to follow went north from City Hall to Grand Central Station,

192
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:54,820
across 42nd Street to Times Square, then north on Broadway to 145th Street,

193
00:18:55,420 --> 00:18:59,660
with a branch leaving Broadway at 105th Street through the Bronx to the Zoo.

194
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:03,540
The more obvious route, straight up Broadway from City Hall,

195
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:06,480
had been opposed by the business owners along Lower Broadway.

196
00:19:07,900 --> 00:19:11,700
The ground under New York streets, like that of most major cities,

197
00:19:11,701 --> 00:19:16,860
was laced with utilities, a vast and in many cases uncharted network of water mains and

198
00:19:16,861 --> 00:19:19,872
sewer pipes, gas lines and telephone cables.

199
00:19:19,873 --> 00:19:23,020
In London, they handled this problem by boring

200
00:19:23,021 --> 00:19:25,269
deep tunnels underneath the whole mess.

201
00:19:25,270 --> 00:19:27,980
But Parsons had been impressed by an innovative

202
00:19:27,981 --> 00:19:32,508
construction technique pioneered in Budapest called cut and cover.

203
00:19:32,509 --> 00:19:33,880
Essentially, cut and

204
00:19:33,881 --> 00:19:38,420
cover means digging a giant trench down the middle of the street, building a box to hold

205
00:19:38,421 --> 00:19:43,600
the subway tracks, then covering it all up with a new street. Of course, all this is

206
00:19:43,601 --> 00:19:47,391
easier to accomplish on land that is undeveloped.

207
00:19:47,392 --> 00:19:50,640
In New York City, a highly developed area,

208
00:19:50,820 --> 00:19:56,420
mile after mile of utilities had to be temporarily relocated while the subway was being built.

209
00:19:57,440 --> 00:20:01,460
Here, for example, a gas main is carried up and over the construction site on its own suspension

210
00:20:01,461 --> 00:20:02,161
bridge.

211
00:20:02,162 --> 00:20:07,820
Once the subway was in place, the utilities were relocated underground, often

212
00:20:07,821 --> 00:20:09,280
with considerable ingenuity.

213
00:20:10,180 --> 00:20:13,380
This large water main was split into several smaller ones

214
00:20:13,381 --> 00:20:16,440
to reduce its thickness where it passed over the tunnel roof.

215
00:20:20,660 --> 00:20:23,560
Steam power drove the construction machines of the day,

216
00:20:23,680 --> 00:20:27,840
steam-powered rock drills and steam cranes to move material around.

217
00:20:28,720 --> 00:20:32,800
The foundations of many of New York's skyscrapers were dug with steam shovels.

218
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:36,460
But for the more delicate task of threading the subways

219
00:20:36,461 --> 00:20:38,320
through New York's underground utilities,

220
00:20:38,600 --> 00:20:40,320
steam shovels were far too clumsy,

221
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:42,720
so most of the digging was done by hand.

222
00:20:43,480 --> 00:20:45,280
One of the biggest problems was maintaining

223
00:20:45,281 --> 00:20:47,360
some semblance of street traffic above

224
00:20:47,361 --> 00:20:48,960
while construction was going on below.

225
00:20:49,700 --> 00:20:52,260
Most of the excavation was roofed over almost immediately

226
00:20:52,261 --> 00:20:54,040
with a temporary wooden street,

227
00:20:54,041 --> 00:20:56,320
and then the work of relocating utilities

228
00:20:56,321 --> 00:20:58,800
and building the tunnel was carried on underneath.

229
00:21:03,940 --> 00:21:06,020
Parsons believed the short-term difficulties

230
00:21:06,021 --> 00:21:10,880
of cut and cover were more than offset by the long-term savings in operating elevators

231
00:21:10,881 --> 00:21:18,240
for deep tunnels. Stations were also brighter and airier.

232
00:21:18,241 --> 00:21:22,460
Along the few sections of subway where deep tunnels were necessary, it's easier to appreciate

233
00:21:22,461 --> 00:21:23,497
Parsons' foresight.

234
00:21:23,498 --> 00:21:27,480
Here in Washington Heights, for example, the subway is 150 feet below the

235
00:21:27,481 --> 00:21:31,340
surface.

236
00:21:31,341 --> 00:21:35,860
While elevator access is perfectly acceptable in a neighborhood station like this, it simply

237
00:21:35,861 --> 00:21:39,980
couldn't handle the volume of people at a midtown station during rush hour.

238
00:21:45,600 --> 00:21:49,380
This section of the subway was the deepest part of the first line.

239
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:53,500
Conventional hard rock mining techniques were used,

240
00:21:53,640 --> 00:21:55,540
relying mainly on the power of explosives.

241
00:21:59,960 --> 00:22:03,480
In fact, the use of explosives was common during subway construction,

242
00:22:03,481 --> 00:22:06,940
and inevitably, despite all the precautions, there were accidents.

243
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:11,240
The events depicted in this 1904 comic short

244
00:22:11,241 --> 00:22:14,520
were familiar to many turn-of-the-century New Yorkers

245
00:22:14,521 --> 00:22:17,640
who found the story amusing and the special effects amazing.

246
00:22:18,360 --> 00:22:19,940
By the way, the title of this film is

247
00:22:19,941 --> 00:22:24,440
City Hall to Harlem in 15 Seconds via the Subway Route.

248
00:22:52,391 --> 00:22:55,340
But the comedy covered up a darker reality.

249
00:22:55,341 --> 00:22:59,120
Explosions were the most common cause of subway construction fatalities.

250
00:22:59,121 --> 00:23:06,620
At 125th Street, faced with the problem of soft water-saturated earth at tunnel level,

251
00:23:06,621 --> 00:23:09,620
Parsons opted to build a viaduct over the area.

252
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:18,320
Many of the construction techniques pioneered during the building of the IRT are still used today.

253
00:23:19,020 --> 00:23:23,740
For his crossing under the Harlem River, Parsons built the tunnel on land like a ship,

254
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:28,360
then towed it out and sank it into a trench that had been dredged into the soft bottom.

255
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:35,360
For the construction of the 63rd Street Tunnel in 1970, a shipyard in Maryland was contracted

256
00:23:35,361 --> 00:23:49,361
to build the tubes which were then towed to New York Harbor by tugboat.

257
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:52,920
It's important to remember that at the turn of the century, all these techniques were

258
00:23:52,921 --> 00:23:58,560
new and yet the subway was completed virtually on time, within budget, and with very little

259
00:23:58,561 --> 00:24:03,800
loss of life.

260
00:24:03,801 --> 00:24:08,900
But the key technology that made the subway possible was the perfection of electric traction

261
00:24:08,901 --> 00:24:10,640
for moving the cars.

262
00:24:10,641 --> 00:24:14,640
Steam locomotives like those used on the elevators had been tried in London, but the smoke and

263
00:24:14,641 --> 00:24:18,180
steam made them impractical underground.

264
00:24:18,181 --> 00:24:24,720
By the 1890s, electric trolleys had replaced horse cars and cable cars nearly everywhere.

265
00:24:24,721 --> 00:24:28,460
This started a boom in building new lines that would not have been practical using any

266
00:24:28,461 --> 00:24:30,020
other kind of power.

267
00:24:30,021 --> 00:24:35,480
Boston was already operating an electric trolley line underground.

268
00:24:35,481 --> 00:24:40,400
But the real breakthrough was multiple unit control, which allowed a motorman in the front

269
00:24:40,401 --> 00:24:43,860
car to control all the motors in a train.

270
00:24:43,861 --> 00:24:50,860
and then on a train of any length had the same performance as a single car.

271
00:25:03,640 --> 00:25:07,900
The interborough's cars were built with no exposed wood to reduce the danger of fire

272
00:25:07,901 --> 00:25:08,882
in the tunnels.

273
00:25:08,883 --> 00:25:13,860
After the first batch of these so-called composite cars, only all steel cars

274
00:25:13,861 --> 00:25:20,860
were ordered, making the subway the first railroad in the world to be equipped with all steel passenger cars.

275
00:25:20,861 --> 00:25:26,860
Power for the cars came from the interborough's own power plant, the largest in the Western Hemisphere.

276
00:25:26,861 --> 00:25:40,861
At the time, there were no power companies as such from which to buy power.

277
00:25:42,860 --> 00:25:46,400
Perhaps the most innovative aspect of the new system had to do with operations.

278
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:51,640
The Elevateds had added a single express track for rush hour operations,

279
00:25:52,060 --> 00:25:54,540
but from the start the new subway had four tracks

280
00:25:54,541 --> 00:25:57,520
and offered both express and local service all day long.

281
00:25:58,100 --> 00:26:00,280
This increased capacity significantly.

282
00:26:03,300 --> 00:26:06,560
The Elevateds had also installed signaling systems

283
00:26:06,561 --> 00:26:08,960
to allow safer operation at higher speeds,

284
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:11,640
but wrecks still occasionally occurred

285
00:26:11,641 --> 00:26:16,520
since it was nothing to prevent a negligent engineer or motorman from passing a red signal.

286
00:26:17,660 --> 00:26:21,320
The new subway had both signals and a trip mechanism

287
00:26:21,321 --> 00:26:26,260
that would apply full emergency brakes if a motorman tried to run a red light.

288
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:33,040
It may come as a surprise to know that in nearly 100 years since the New York subway was first built,

289
00:26:33,160 --> 00:26:35,300
the technology has changed very little.

290
00:26:35,301 --> 00:26:42,300
The same shops that service today's cars keep the restored museum trains in tip-top shape.

291
00:26:51,300 --> 00:26:56,300
One might think that computers are now used to avoid the delays that still regularly annoy riders,

292
00:26:56,301 --> 00:27:01,300
but experience has shown that no computer yet programmed can replace the quick reflexes

293
00:27:01,301 --> 00:27:05,800
reflexes of the dispatchers and controllers who supervise the system today.

294
00:27:11,700 --> 00:27:18,180
The MetroCard Gold was launched on July 4, 1997, ending the two-fair zone by offering

295
00:27:18,181 --> 00:27:22,940
free transfers between subways and buses and buses and subways within a two-hour period.

296
00:27:23,740 --> 00:27:25,460
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

297
00:27:27,180 --> 00:27:31,180
Some of America's best-known fortunes were made in railroads.

298
00:27:31,300 --> 00:27:34,020
Vanderbilt, Huntington, Harriman, and Jay Gould,

299
00:27:34,280 --> 00:27:36,760
at one time perhaps the most powerful man in America.

300
00:27:37,840 --> 00:27:40,560
He controlled the Union Pacific, the Missouri Pacific,

301
00:27:40,860 --> 00:27:42,360
the Western Union Telegraph Company,

302
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:45,220
and the little Manhattan Elevated.

303
00:27:46,040 --> 00:27:48,640
Not so strange, considering that it carried more passengers

304
00:27:48,641 --> 00:27:50,580
than all his other railroads put together.

305
00:27:51,320 --> 00:27:54,780
So there was no lack of investor interest in New York's subway.

306
00:27:54,781 --> 00:27:59,220
The problem, as with any new undertaking, was investor fear.

307
00:28:01,300 --> 00:28:02,000
Abram S.

308
00:28:02,001 --> 00:28:07,700
Hewitt, former mayor of New York, addressed the problem by inventing the municipal bond.

309
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:14,660
Commonplace today, in 1900, it was a revolutionary new way to finance large public works projects.

310
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:16,999
The contractor was John B.

311
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:22,020
MacDonald, who had been in charge of some of the country's major railroad building projects.

312
00:28:22,520 --> 00:28:25,120
But even he had trouble raising the needed security.

313
00:28:25,920 --> 00:28:30,200
Eventually, he was forced to turn the contract over to financier August Belmont, Jr.,

314
00:28:30,201 --> 00:28:32,580
the American representative of the Rothschilds.

315
00:28:33,040 --> 00:28:37,080
Belmont put together the financial package and hired McDonald to build the subway.

316
00:28:37,900 --> 00:28:42,840
He set up a separate company, the Interborough Rapid Transit Company, or IRT,

317
00:28:42,841 --> 00:28:45,160
to lease and operate the lines.

318
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:51,320
When the subway opened, the investors' dreams were fully realized.

319
00:28:51,500 --> 00:28:53,560
Ridership was high, so were profits.

320
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:57,700
Belmont liked to show off the subway as if it were his very own.

321
00:28:58,340 --> 00:29:02,480
To underscore this impression, he commissioned a private subway car, the Mineola.

322
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:07,640
It was equipped with an office, lounge, lavatory, bar, and kitchen.

323
00:29:08,160 --> 00:29:12,780
The interior was finished in mahogany with velvet drapes and stained glass windows.

324
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:17,560
It remains to this day the only private rapid transit car ever built.

325
00:29:17,561 --> 00:29:25,720
Belmont liked to take guests from a special sighting in the basement of his Belmont Hotel

326
00:29:25,721 --> 00:29:27,300
across from Grand Central Station

327
00:29:27,301 --> 00:29:31,800
to Belmont Park race course on Long Island

328
00:29:31,801 --> 00:29:37,140
A private railroad car is not an acquired taste

329
00:29:37,141 --> 00:29:38,920
One takes to it immediately

330
00:29:38,921 --> 00:29:41,080
Mrs. August Belmont

331
00:29:41,081 --> 00:29:53,480
With the huge success of the IRT, every railroad operator in the New York area wanted a piece of the lucrative subway pie.

332
00:29:53,481 --> 00:30:02,720
The Long Island Railroad, the New York Central, and the Hudson and Manhattan all tried unsuccessfully to wrest the subway monopoly away from the IRT.

333
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:07,840
The outfit that finally succeeded was the BRT, or Brooklyn Rapid Transit,

334
00:30:08,040 --> 00:30:11,640
operator of Brooklyn's vast network of trolley and elevated lines.

335
00:30:12,460 --> 00:30:18,100
The BRT's most distant precursor was the Brooklyn, Bath and Coney Island Railroad.

336
00:30:18,660 --> 00:30:22,120
In 1866, it opened a line from Brooklyn to Coney Island,

337
00:30:22,121 --> 00:30:24,360
already a popular seaside resort.

338
00:30:24,361 --> 00:30:31,280
in 1875

339
00:30:31,281 --> 00:30:34,540
came the Prospect Park and Coney Island

340
00:30:34,541 --> 00:30:39,440
the Brooklyn Flatbush and Coney Island

341
00:30:39,441 --> 00:30:41,020
opened in 1878

342
00:30:41,021 --> 00:30:45,200
in 1879 the New York and Sea Beach

343
00:30:45,201 --> 00:30:50,100
in 1881 the Coney Island Elevated Railway

344
00:30:50,101 --> 00:30:53,220
then in 1883

345
00:30:53,221 --> 00:30:58,580
the Brooklyn Bridge opened, bringing a surge in the number of elevated railways connecting with it.

346
00:30:59,300 --> 00:31:02,340
Coney Island was now so accessible it was no longer just a resort,

347
00:31:02,480 --> 00:31:04,420
but a popular destination for day trips.

348
00:31:05,080 --> 00:31:09,320
Amusement attractions of all kinds began to vie for the day tripper's business.

349
00:31:23,220 --> 00:31:37,220
The area between downtown Brooklyn and Coney Island began to develop.

350
00:31:41,820 --> 00:31:44,580
As more electric trolleys were built in the late 1880s,

351
00:31:45,560 --> 00:31:49,940
a dense network of streetcar lines appeared to fill in the gaps between steam lines.

352
00:31:49,941 --> 00:31:56,940
With the development of multiple unit control, all the steam routes were electrified between 1893 and 1900.

353
00:31:56,941 --> 00:32:02,940
The lines of the four original steam railroads to Coney Island still ran mostly along surface tracks,

354
00:32:02,941 --> 00:32:08,940
so during the early years of the new century, the railroads began a systematic program of grade separation.

355
00:32:08,941 --> 00:32:14,940
By the turn of the century, nearly all of this was controlled by the Brooklyn Rapid Transit.

356
00:32:19,940 --> 00:32:26,420
By 1911, the famous dual contracts between the city and the IRT and the BRT

357
00:32:26,421 --> 00:32:30,660
signaled the start of New York's greatest period of subway expansion.

358
00:32:39,580 --> 00:32:43,080
This second round of subway building also had its share of accidents.

359
00:32:43,260 --> 00:32:46,120
This cave-in was on 7th Avenue at 23rd Street.

360
00:32:46,820 --> 00:32:50,460
Luckily, the streetcar that made a detour here into the subway

361
00:32:50,461 --> 00:32:53,060
was running empty back to the car barn.

362
00:32:53,520 --> 00:32:56,520
Its motorman and conductor walked away unharmed.

363
00:32:58,620 --> 00:33:02,740
The most serious accident, however, had nothing to do with the construction.

364
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:06,740
On November 1, 1918, an inexperienced motorman

365
00:33:06,741 --> 00:33:10,000
headed across the Brooklyn Bridge bound for Brighton Beach.

366
00:33:10,560 --> 00:33:14,660
A yard motorman pressed into evening rush hour service because of a strike,

367
00:33:14,661 --> 00:33:17,800
he had difficulty controlling his train of ancient wooden cars

368
00:33:17,801 --> 00:33:20,100
as he traveled along the Fulton Avenue Elevated.

369
00:33:20,860 --> 00:33:22,160
He turned off at Franklin,

370
00:33:22,380 --> 00:33:24,900
onto the tracks of what is now the Franklin Avenue Shuttle,

371
00:33:24,980 --> 00:33:27,500
and descended into the tunnel at Malbone Street,

372
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:30,260
unwittingly doing 45 miles an hour,

373
00:33:31,020 --> 00:33:33,760
heading into an S-curve that was only safe at 6.

374
00:33:44,660 --> 00:33:54,820
97 people died.

375
00:33:55,340 --> 00:33:56,720
250 were injured.

376
00:33:57,480 --> 00:34:00,440
It remains the worst rapid transit disaster in history.

377
00:34:05,320 --> 00:34:10,500
The New York Transit Museum is located in an authentic decommissioned IND station

378
00:34:10,501 --> 00:34:12,280
in downtown Brooklyn, New York.

379
00:34:12,281 --> 00:34:17,280
There, among the exhibits, is the first turnstile, built in 1922.

380
00:34:17,281 --> 00:34:20,280
These turnstiles were removed from use in 1993.

381
00:34:20,281 --> 00:34:23,280
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

382
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:51,920
The Independence large cars look strictly utilitarian compared with today's stainless steel,

383
00:34:51,921 --> 00:34:55,460
but at the time they were considered state-of-the-art and handsome.

384
00:34:56,720 --> 00:35:00,480
For the first time, subways directly targeted elevated lines,

385
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:04,800
building under 6th Avenue in Manhattan and under Fulton Street in Brooklyn.

386
00:35:05,400 --> 00:35:08,800
The city was turning up the pressure on the private transit operators.

387
00:35:12,280 --> 00:35:18,120
In some places, construction was simple.

388
00:35:18,121 --> 00:35:23,280
Here under broad Queens Boulevard, where underground utilities were not very dense, construction

389
00:35:23,281 --> 00:35:27,180
was nearly as easy as building through farmland.

390
00:35:27,181 --> 00:35:31,800
But in Brooklyn, the route of the Independent was complicated by a labyrinth of underground

391
00:35:31,801 --> 00:35:42,220
junctions for the BMT and IRT that already existed around Flatbush and Atlantic Avenue.

392
00:35:42,221 --> 00:35:47,440
In Manhattan, under 6th Avenue, near 34th Street, the line threaded over an aqueduct

393
00:35:47,441 --> 00:35:53,380
and the Pennsylvania and Long Island railroads, and under the Hudson and Manhattan, the Broadway

394
00:35:53,381 --> 00:35:56,720
BMT, the street, and the 6th Avenue elevated.

395
00:35:57,400 --> 00:36:00,660
It was the most difficult subway construction ever attempted.

396
00:36:03,060 --> 00:36:06,560
The Independent began opening lines in 1932.

397
00:36:06,561 --> 00:36:11,100
Its last major line, the 6th Avenue trunk, opened in 1940.

398
00:36:12,220 --> 00:36:16,340
Competition from the Independent added to the lingering effects of the Depression

399
00:36:16,341 --> 00:36:20,380
to force the IRT and the BMT into receivership,

400
00:36:20,880 --> 00:36:23,640
setting the stage for city takeover in 1940.

401
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:34,020
For the first time in a century, Manhattan had excess north-south transportation capacity,

402
00:36:34,021 --> 00:36:36,900
and the demolition of the elevators began.

403
00:36:42,220 --> 00:36:48,660
Most of the 6th Avenue went in 1938, the 9th Avenue in 1940, and the 2nd Avenue in 1942.

404
00:36:52,560 --> 00:36:58,800
The 3rd Avenue L survived through World War II, when demand for public transit hit an all-time peak.

405
00:36:59,240 --> 00:37:01,680
But it, too, was torn down in 1955.

406
00:37:02,900 --> 00:37:09,280
Now, with only the Lexington Avenue subway line serving the east side, overcrowding is a big problem,

407
00:37:09,281 --> 00:37:12,840
And there are some who actually miss the old L.

408
00:37:16,060 --> 00:37:20,760
This is not to say that there were no subway extensions, but all were conversions,

409
00:37:21,320 --> 00:37:26,600
taking railroad lines previously operated by other outfits and converting them for use by subway trains.

410
00:37:26,601 --> 00:37:40,601
but the real post-world war ii transportation story in the new york metropolitan area is that

411
00:37:42,020 --> 00:37:48,720
of a remarkable man robert moses moses used his position as the city's coordinator of construction

412
00:37:48,721 --> 00:37:56,320
to build a vast empire of bridges tunnels parkways and expressways he was a master builder and a

413
00:37:56,321 --> 00:38:02,160
master politician, skilled at attracting federal construction grants as well as local funds

414
00:38:02,161 --> 00:38:05,179
that might otherwise have been spent on subways.

415
00:38:05,180 --> 00:38:08,260
Moses successfully funneled all the toll revenues

416
00:38:08,261 --> 00:38:11,205
of his facilities into more highway projects.

417
00:38:11,206 --> 00:38:14,280
He insulated himself from public accountability

418
00:38:14,281 --> 00:38:18,570
by operating behind a protective screen of quasi-public authorities.

419
00:38:18,571 --> 00:38:19,580
During the first

420
00:38:19,581 --> 00:38:26,700
25 years after World War II, he built 627 miles of superhighway in the New York metropolitan area.

421
00:38:27,480 --> 00:38:30,520
Meanwhile, not a mile of new subway was built.

422
00:38:31,880 --> 00:38:36,180
Moses used his influence to block construction of the long-planned Second Avenue subway.

423
00:38:36,740 --> 00:38:40,480
It was only after he left the scene that bits and pieces of the subway were begun,

424
00:38:40,980 --> 00:38:45,640
but even today we seem to lack the political will, the economic muscle to tie the pieces together.

425
00:38:45,641 --> 00:38:48,509
There have been some signs of progress.

426
00:38:48,510 --> 00:38:54,100
In October 1989, the 63rd Street tunnel from Manhattan to Queens was opened.

427
00:38:55,840 --> 00:39:00,660
The Moses era was a period of decline, decay and neglect for the subways.

428
00:39:01,260 --> 00:39:07,680
Unable to expand to meet demand, short of operating funds, the subways began a policy of deferred maintenance.

429
00:39:08,300 --> 00:39:10,640
Cars, tracks and stations began to deteriorate.

430
00:39:10,641 --> 00:39:18,720
It became official Transit Authority policy not to wash subway cars between repaintings as a rust prevention measure.

431
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:23,700
Then, in 1970, came graffiti.

432
00:39:24,700 --> 00:39:30,380
It's art, said some, and it was arguably an improvement over the uniform Transit Authority grunge.

433
00:39:30,960 --> 00:39:39,460
But as the blight spread, graffiti became a constant reminder to New Yorkers that their subway and city were out of control.

434
00:39:40,640 --> 00:39:46,120
The graffiti prompted the Transit Authority to take action and the effort has met with

435
00:39:46,121 --> 00:39:48,360
some highly visible success.

436
00:39:48,361 --> 00:40:02,361
All trains are now graffiti free as well as air conditioned.

437
00:40:07,180 --> 00:40:09,460
Maintenance is once again a priority.

438
00:40:09,461 --> 00:40:23,461
and the wheels are getting the flat spots turned out of them, so they'll run more quietly.

439
00:40:27,700 --> 00:40:32,700
By banishing outlaw art, people are better able to appreciate the more conventional subway

440
00:40:32,701 --> 00:40:33,960
art.

441
00:40:33,961 --> 00:40:40,640
The original IRT contract allotted $500,000 for decorating the stations with tile, mosaics,

442
00:40:40,641 --> 00:40:41,640
and bas-reliefs.

443
00:40:41,641 --> 00:40:46,900
They will have their effect on public taste just the same as anything else that tends

444
00:40:46,901 --> 00:40:49,160
to uplift and refine.

445
00:40:49,161 --> 00:40:51,300
IRT brochure.

446
00:40:51,301 --> 00:40:55,080
And the subway has figured in artistic expression ever since it opened.

447
00:40:55,081 --> 00:40:58,760
This song voiced a fear that seems oddly appropriate even today.

448
00:41:03,960 --> 00:41:07,380
He married her that morning, that night.

449
00:41:07,520 --> 00:41:09,200
He had no bride at all.

450
00:41:09,260 --> 00:41:10,920
Just think of his dilemma.

451
00:41:11,500 --> 00:41:13,360
No honeymoon that day.

452
00:41:13,720 --> 00:41:17,660
Oh, me, oh, my, I could cry.

453
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:19,300
He loved him, he owned Subway.

454
00:41:23,120 --> 00:41:25,040
Less than a year after it opened,

455
00:41:25,120 --> 00:41:28,220
the Subway began a long career as a setting for the movies.

456
00:41:28,880 --> 00:41:31,980
Once again, notice the similarities between today's concerns

457
00:41:31,981 --> 00:41:33,840
and those of 1905.

458
00:42:01,980 --> 00:42:05,380
From the beginning, the subway captured the public imagination

459
00:42:05,381 --> 00:42:09,660
and so became the incubator for a unique culture beneath the streets.

460
00:42:10,460 --> 00:42:14,720
Each day, the subway becomes a stage for all kinds of artistic expression,

461
00:42:15,220 --> 00:42:18,840
the underground cultural crossroads of a city.

462
00:42:53,820 --> 00:42:58,980
What a week for New York City's modern marvels here on the History Channel.

463
00:42:58,981 --> 00:43:04,660
We've ridden elevators up into the towers that scraped the sky, crossed the great bridges,

464
00:43:04,661 --> 00:43:08,900
pounded out copper for the Statue of Liberty, and rode through the underground labyrinths

465
00:43:08,901 --> 00:43:10,700
of the subway system.

466
00:43:10,701 --> 00:43:12,740
But seeing it on television is one thing.

467
00:43:12,741 --> 00:43:17,720
There's simply no replacement for coming here and experiencing it all for yourself.

468
00:43:17,721 --> 00:43:20,220
I'm Dave Dinkins, and thanks for joining us.

